. Cited by: cite146†§1 .\nL399:   * Yao et al. (2022) S. Yao, H. Chen, J. Yang, and K. Narasimhan Webshop: towards scalable real-world web interaction with grounded language agents. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 35, pp. 20744–20757. Cited by: cite146†§1 .\nL400:   * Yin et al. (2024) Y. Yin, Z. Wang, Y. Gu, H. Huang, W. Chen, and M. Zhou Relative preference optimization: enhancing llm alignment through contrasting responses across identical and diverse prompts. arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.10958. Cited by: cite142†§1 , cite153†2nd item .\nL401:   * Yuan et al. (2025) X. Yuan, X. Chen, T. Yu, D. Shi, C. Jin, W. Lee, and S. Mitra Mitigating forgetting between supervised and reinforcement learning yields stronger reasoners. arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.04454. Cited by: cite139†§1 .\nL402:   * Zelikman et al. (2022) E. Zelikman, Y. Wu, J. Mu, and N. Goodman Star: bootstrapping reasoning with reasoning. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 35, pp. 15476–15488. Cited by: cite146†§1 .\nL403:   * Zhang et al. (2023) Y. Zhang, J. Yang, Y. Yuan, and A. C. Yao Cumulative reasoning with large language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2308.04371. Cited by: cite156†1st item .\nL404:   * Zhao et al. (2026) S. Zhao, Z. Xie, M. Liu, J. Huang, G. Pang, F. Chen, and A. Grover Self-distilled reasoner: on-policy self-distillation for large language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.18734. Cited by: cite148†§3.2 .\nL405:   * Zhao et al. (2023) W. X. Zhao, K. Zhou, J. Li, T. Tang, X. Wang, Y. Hou, Y. Min, B. Zhang, J. Zhang, Z. Dong, et al. A survey of large language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.18223 1 (2). Cited by: cite146†§1 .\nL406:   * Zhu et al. (2025) H. Zhu, J. Su, P. Lai, R. Ma, W. Zhang, L. Yang, and G. Chen Anchored supervised fine-tuning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.23753. Cited by: cite151†§1 .\nL407: ## Appendix A Distribution Discriminant Theory\nL408: \nL409: ### A.1 Derivation of Signal-to-Noise Ratio Improvement\nL410: \nL411: In this appendix, we provide a rigorous derivation demonstrating that the proposed Centered Log-Likelihood (CLL) statistic theoretically maximizes the Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) compared to the standard Perplexity (LL) baseline. We employ the Law of Total Variance to decompose the uncertainty sources into context-level variability and token-level intrinsic noise.\nL412: #### A.1.1 Problem Setup and Definitions\nL413: \nL414: Let $c$ denote a context sampled from the data distribution $\\mathcal{D}$, and $x$ denote a token generated conditioned on $c$. We define two sources of randomness:\nL415: \nL416:   1. 1.\nL417: \nL418: Context Sampling: $c\\sim\\mathcal{D}$. The entropy of the model’s prediction given $c$ is $H(c)\\coloneqq\\mathbb{E}_{x\\sim p(\\cdot|c)}[-\\log p(x|c)]$.\nL419: \nL420:   2. 2.\nL421: Token Generation: $x\\sim p(\\cdot|c)$ under the null hypothesis $\\mathcal{H}_{0}$ (In-Distribution), and $x\\sim q(\\cdot|c)$ under the alternative hypothesis $\\mathcal{H}_{1}$ (Out-of-Distribution).\nL422: \nL423: We define the Global Signal-to-Noise Ratio for a detection statistic $S$ as:\nL424: \nL425:  | $$\\text{SNR}(S)\\coloneqq\\frac{\\left(\\mathbb{E}_{c,x}[S\\mid\\mathcal{H}_{1}]-\\mathbb{E}_{c,x}[S\\mid\\mathcal{H}_{0}]\\right)^{2}}{\\mathrm{Var}_{c,x}(S\\mid\\mathcal{H}_{0})}.$$  |  | (6)\nL426: #### A.1.2 Analysis of the Baseline (LL)\nL427: \nL428: The standard LL statistic is defined as the raw log-likelihood: $S_{\\text{LL}}(x,c)=\\log p(x|c)$.\nL429: #### A.1.3 Variance Decomposition (The Denominator)\nL430: \nL431: Using the Law of Total Variance, we decompose the variance of $S_{\\text{LL}}$ under $\\mathcal{H}_{0}$ into inter-context and intra-context components:\nL432: \nL433:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Var}(S_{\\text{LL}})$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathrm{Var}_{c}\\left(\\mathbb{E}_{x|c}[S_{\\text{LL}}]\\right)+\\mathbb{E}_{c}\\left[\\mathrm{Var}_{x|c}(S_{\\text{LL}})\\right].$  |  | (7)\nL434: \nL435: Analyzing the first term (Inter-Context Variance):\nL436:  | $$\\mathbb{E}_{x|c}[S_{\\text{LL}}]=\\mathbb{E}_{x\\sim p(\\cdot|c)}[\\log p(x|c)]=-H(c).$$  |  | (8)\nL437: \nL438: Thus, the first term becomes $\\mathrm{Var}_{c}(-H(c))=\\sigma_{H}^{2}$, which represents the variability of difficulty across contexts.\nL439: Analyzing the second term (Intra-Context Variance): Let $\\sigma_{\\epsilon}^{2}(c)\\coloneqq\\mathrm{Var}_{x\\sim p(\\cdot|c)}(\\log p(x|c))$ be the intrinsic aleatoric noise for context $c$. The second term is the average intrinsic noise $\\bar{\\sigma}_{\\epsilon}^{2}=\\mathbb{E}_{c}[\\sigma_{\\epsilon}^{2}(c)]$.\nL440: \nL441: Substituting these back, the total noise for LL is:\nL442: \nL443:  | $$\\mathrm{Noise}^{2}_{\\text{LL}}=\\sigma_{H}^{2}+\\bar{\\sigma}_{\\epsilon}^{2}.$$  |  | (9)\nL444: #### A.1.4 Signal Analysis (The Numerator)\nL445: \nL446: The expected drift under distribution shift is:\nL447: \nL448:  | $\\displaystyle\\Delta_{\\text{LL}}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{c,x}[S_{\\text{LL}}|\\mathcal{H}_{1}]-\\mathbb{E}_{c,x}[S_{\\text{LL}}|\\mathcal{H}_{0}]$  |\nL449:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{c}[-H(q,p)]-\\mathbb{E}_{c}[-H(p)]$  |\nL450:  |  | $\\displaystyle=-\\mathbb{E}_{c}[\\mathrm{KL}(q(\\cdot|c)\\,\\|\\,p(\\cdot|c))].$  |  | (10)\nL451: \nL452: Let $\\Delta=\\mathbb{E}_{c}[\\mathrm{KL}(q\\|p)]$. The squared signal is $\\Delta^{2}$.\nL453: Resulting SNR for LL:\nL454: \nL455:  | $$\\text{SNR}_{\\text{LL}}=\\frac{\\Delta^{2}}{\\sigma_{H}^{2}+\\bar{\\sigma}_{\\epsilon}^{2}}.$$  |  | (11)\nL456: #### A.1.5 Analysis of Proposed Method (CLL)\nL457: \nL458: We define the Centered Log-Likelihood as $\\varphi(x,c)=\\log p(x|c)+H(c)$. Here, $H(c)$ acts as a context-dependent control variate.\nL459: #### A.1.6 Variance Reduction\nL460: \nL461: ALLying the Law of Total Variance to $\\varphi$ under $\\mathcal{H}_{0}$:\nL462: \nL463:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathrm{Var}(\\varphi)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathrm{Var}_{c}\\left(\\mathbb{E}_{x|c}[\\varphi]\\right)+\\mathbb{E}_{c}\\left[\\mathrm{Var}_{x|c}(\\varphi)\\right].$  |  | (12)\nL464: \nL465: Crucially, under $\\mathcal{H}_{0}$, the conditional expectation is centered at zero for any context $c$:\nL466: \nL467:  | $$\\mathbb{E}_{x|c}[\\log p(x|c)+H(c)]=-H(c)+H(c)=0.$$  |  | (13)\nL468: Since the conditional mean is the constant $0$, its variance is zero:\nL469: \nL470:  | $$\\mathrm{Var}_{c}\\left(\\mathbb{E}_{x|c}[\\varphi]\\right)=\\mathrm{Var}_{c}(0)=0.$$  |  | (14)\nL471: \nL472: For the second term, since $H(c)$ is constant given $c$, it does not affect the conditional variance:\nL473: \nL474:  | $$\\mathrm{Var}_{x|c}(\\log p(x|c)+H(c))=\\mathrm{Var}_{x|c}(\\log p(x|c))=\\sigma_{\\epsilon}^{2}(c).$$  |  | (15)\nL475: \nL476: Thus, the total noise for CLL is significantly reduced:\nL477:  | $$\\mathrm{Noise}^{2}_{\\text{CLL}}=0+\\bar{\\sigma}_{\\epsilon}^{2}=\\bar{\\sigma}_{\\epsilon}^{2}.$$  |  | (16)\nL478: #### A.1.7 Signal Preservation\nL479: \nL480: We verify that the signal magnitude is preserved. The term $H(c)$ cancels out when taking the difference of expectations:\nL481: \nL482:  | $\\displaystyle\\Delta_{\\text{CLL}}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi|\\mathcal{H}_{1}]-\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi|\\mathcal{H}_{0}]$  |\nL483:  |  | $\\displaystyle=(\\mathbb{E}[S_{\\text{LL}}|\\mathcal{H}_{1}]+\\mathbb{E}[H])-(\\mathbb{E}[S_{\\text{LL}}|\\mathcal{H}_{0}]+\\mathbb{E}[H])$  |\nL484:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\Delta_{\\text{LL}}=-\\Delta.$  |  | (17)\nL485: The squared signal remains $\\Delta^{2}$.\nL486: #### A.1.8 Conclusion\nL487: \nL488: Comparing the derived SNRs:\nL489: \nL490:  | $$\\text{SNR}_{\\text{CLL}}=\\frac{\\Delta^{2}}{\\bar{\\sigma}_{\\epsilon}^{2}}\\quad\\text{vs.}\\quad\\text{SNR}_{\\text{LL}}=\\frac{\\Delta^{2}}{\\sigma_{H}^{2}+\\bar{\\sigma}_{\\epsilon}^{2}}.$$  |  | (18)\nL491: \nL492: Since natural language datasets exhibit diverse complexity, implying $\\sigma_{H}^{2}>0$, we conclude that:\nL493: \nL494:  | $$\\text{SNR}_{\\text{CLL}}>\\text{SNR}_{\\text{LL}}.$$  |  | (19)\nL495: This proves that the CLL statistic strictly dominates the LL baseline in terms of Signal-to-Noise Ratio by eliminating the variance contribution from context difficulty.\nL496: ### A.2 Mathematical Proofs of Sequential Properties\nL497: \nL498: In this section, we provide rigorous derivations for the stochastic properties of the cumulative statistic $S_{n}=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\varphi_{t}$, where $\\varphi_{t}=\\log p_{t}(x_{t})+H(p_{t})$.\nL499: \nL500: #### A.2.1 Proof of Martingale Property under $\\mathcal{H}_{0}$\nL501: ###### Proposition A.1.\nL502: \nL503: Under the in-distribution hypothesis $\\mathcal{H}_{0}$ (where $x_{t}\\sim p_{t}$), the sequence $\\{S_{n}\\}_{n\\geq 0}$ is a discrete-time martingale with respect to the filtration $\\mathcal{F}_{n}=\\sigma(x_{1},\\dots,x_{n})$.\nL504: \nL505: Proof. A sequence is a martingale if $\\mathbb{E}[|S_{n}|]<\\infty$ and $\\mathbb{E}[S_{n}\\mid\\mathcal{F}_{n-1}]=S_{n-1}$. Consider the increment $\\Delta S_{n}=S_{n}-S_{n-1}=\\varphi_{t}$. We calculate its conditional expectation under $\\mathcal{H}_{0}$:\nL506:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi_{t}\\mid\\mathcal{F}_{n-1},\\mathcal{H}_{0}]$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{x_{t}\\sim p_{t}}[\\log p_{t}(x_{t})+H(p_{t})]$  |\nL507:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{x_{t}\\sim p_{t}}[\\log p_{t}(x_{t})]+H(p_{t})$  |\nL508:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal{V}}p_{t}(x)\\log p_{t}(x)+H(p_{t})$  |\nL509:  |  | $\\displaystyle=-H(p_{t})+H(p_{t})$  |\nL510:  |  | $\\displaystyle=0.$  |  | (20)\nL511: \nL512: Since the expected increment is zero, we have:\nL513:  | $$\\mathbb{E}[S_{n}\\mid\\mathcal{F}_{n-1}]=S_{n-1}+\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi_{t}\\mid\\mathcal{F}_{n-1}]=S_{n-1}.$$  |  | (21)\nL514: \nL515: Thus, $S_{n}$ is a zero-mean martingale under $\\mathcal{H}_{0}$. $\\square$\nL516: #### A.2.2 Proof of Negative Drift under $\\mathcal{H}_{1}$\nL517: ###### Proposition A.2.\nL518: \nL519: Under the out-of-distribution hypothesis $\\mathcal{H}_{1}$ (where $x_{t}\\sim q_{t}\\neq p_{t}$), the statistic $\\varphi_{t}$ has a strictly negative expectation if the KL divergence dominates the entropy gap.\nL520: \nL521: Proof. We calculate the expectation of $\\varphi_{t}$ with respect to the true generating distribution $q_{t}$:\nL522:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi_{t}\\mid\\mathcal{H}_{1}]$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{x_{t}\\sim q_{t}}[\\log p_{t}(x_{t})+H(p_{t})]$  |\nL523:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal{V}}q_{t}(x)\\log p_{t}(x)+H(p_{t}).$  |  | (22)\nL524: \nL525: We rewrite the cross-entropy term $\\sum q\\log p$:\nL526:  | $\\displaystyle\\sum_{x}q_{t}(x)\\log p_{t}(x)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{x}q_{t}(x)\\log\\frac{p_{t}(x)}{q_{t}(x)}q_{t}(x)$  |\nL527:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{x}q_{t}(x)\\log\\frac{p_{t}(x)}{q_{t}(x)}+\\sum_{x}q_{t}(x)\\log q_{t}(x)$  |\nL528:  |  | $\\displaystyle=-D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(q_{t}\\|p_{t})-H(q_{t}).$  |  | (23)\nL529: \nL530: Substituting this back, we obtain:\nL531: \nL532:  | $$\\mathbb{E}[\\varphi_{t}\\mid\\mathcal{H}_{1}]=-D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(q_{t}\\|p_{t})+(H(p_{t})-H(q_{t})).$$  |  | (24)\nL533: Since the KL divergence $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(q_{t}\\|p_{t})\\geq 0$ is typically the dominant term for OOD samples (where the model assigns low probability to the observed distribution), and assuming the entropy difference is bounded or negligible compared to the divergence, the expectation is negative. Specifically, if $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(q_{t}\\|p_{t})>H(p_{t})-H(q_{t})$, then $\\mathbb{E}[\\Delta S_{n}]<0$, causing a downward linear drift. $\\square$\nL534: #### A.2.3 Concentration Inequality and Error Bounds\nL535: \nL536: To theoretically bound the False Positive Rate (Type I Error), we analyze the probability that the cumulative score $S_{L}$ drops below a threshold $-\\lambda$ given that the sequence is actually In-Distribution ($\\mathcal{H}_{0}$).\nL537: ###### Theorem A.3 (Tail Bound for IDFT).\nL538: \nL539: Assume the statistic $\\varphi_{t}$ is bounded such that $|\\varphi_{t}|\\leq c$ for some constant $c$, and let $\\sigma_{t}^{2}=\\mathrm{Var}(\\varphi_{t}\\mid\\mathcal{H}_{0})$. For a sequence of length $L$, the probability of a false rejection is bounded by:\nL540: \nL541:  | $$\\mathbb{P}(S_{L}\\leq-\\lambda\\mid\\mathcal{H}_{0})\\leq\\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\lambda^{2}}{2\\sum_{t=1}^{L}\\sigma_{t}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}c\\lambda}\\right).$$  |  | (25)\nL542: Proof. Since $\\{S_{n}\\}$ is a zero-mean martingale with bounded increments $|\\varphi_{t}|\\leq c$ and conditional variance $\\sigma_{t}^{2}$, we can invoke the Freedman’s Inequality (a martingale variant of Bernstein’s inequality). Freedman’s inequality states that for a martingale difference sequence $X_{t}$ with $X_{t}\\leq c$, and $V_{L}=\\sum\\mathrm{Var}(X_{t}\\mid\\mathcal{F}_{t-1})$, the tail probability is bounded by:\nL543:  | $$\\mathbb{P}\\left(\\sum_{t=1}^{L}X_{t}\\geq\\lambda\\right)\\leq\\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\lambda^{2}}{2V_{L}+2c\\lambda/3}\\right).$$  |  | (26)\nL544: \nL545: ALLying this to our statistic: we are interested in the lower tail $S_{L}\\leq-\\lambda$. By symmetry of the bound (or aLLying to $-S_{L}$), and noting that $V_{L}=\\sum_{t=1}^{L}\\sigma_{t}^{2}$, we directly obtain the stated bound.\nL546: This result implies that the false positive rate decays exponentially with the squared threshold $\\lambda^{2}$, scaled by the accumulated aleatoric variance $\\sum\\sigma_{t}^{2}$. Compared to standard raw log-likelihood (which includes the high variance of entropy $\\sigma_{H}^{2}$ in the denominator), our statistic $\\varphi_{t}$ minimizes the denominator to $\\sum\\sigma_{\\epsilon}^{2}$, thereby significantly tightening the error bound and allowing for more sensitive detection thresholds. $\\square$\nL547: ### A.3 Derivation of the Formula for Hinted Decoding\nL548: \nL549: We start from the variational problem:\nL550: \nL551:  | $$\\max_{q}\\quad-KL(q||p_{I})+E_{q}[\\hat{\\lambda}(H^{I})\\varphi_{m}],$$  |\nL552: \nL553: Using the definition:\nL554:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{J}[q]$  | $\\displaystyle=-KL(q||p_{I})+E_{q}[\\hat{\\lambda}(H^{I})\\varphi_{m}],$  |\nL555:  |  | $\\displaystyle=-\\int q\\cdot\\log\\frac{q}{p_{I}}+\\int q\\cdot\\hat{\\lambda}(H^{I})\\left(\\log p_{m}+H^{m}\\right)$  |\nL556:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\int q\\cdot\\left[\\log p_{I}+\\hat{\\lambda}(H^{I})\\log p_{m}-\\log q\\right]+\\hat{\\lambda}(H^{I})H^{m}$  |\nL557: The objective functional $\\mathcal{J}[q]$ is a strictly concave function with respect to $q$ (when $q$ is at an interior point), because $\\int q\\log q$ is strictly concave. The linear term does not alter the concavity, therefore, the global optimum is uniquely determined by the first-order condition. The Lagrangian function with the normalization constraint can be expressed as follows. Given the constraint $\\int q=1$, the Lagrangian function is defined as:\nL558:  | $$\\mathcal{L}[q,\\alpha]=\\mathcal{J}[q]+\\alpha\\cdot\\left(\\int q-1\\right).$$  |\nL559: \nL560: Now, we take the partial derivative of the Lagrangian function with respect to each component and set it to zero:\nL561: \nL562:  | $$\\frac{\\partial\\mathcal{L}}{\\partial q}=\\hat{\\lambda}(H^{I})\\log p_{m}+\\log p_{I}-\\log q-1+\\alpha=0,$$  |\nL563: \nL564: which gives:\nL565: \nL566:  | $$q=C\\cdot p_{I}\\cdot p_{m}^{\\hat{\\lambda}(H^{I})}.$$  |\nL567: ### A.4 Theoretical Justification for the Logarithmic Transformation\nL568: \nL569: In this appendix, we provide a comprehensive justification for selecting the logarithmic function as the basis for our detection statistic. We first present the physical intuition grounded in the mechanics of neural networks, and then provide a rigorous algebraic proof based on group isomorphism.\nL570: #### A.4.1 Physical Motivation: Multiplicative Noise in LLMs\nL571: \nL572: The assumption that aleatoric uncertainty in language models is multiplicative (rather than additive) is grounded in the fundamental architectural constraints of modern neural networks.\nL573: ##### The Softmax Amplification Mechanism.\nL574: \nL575: Neural networks typically operate in a continuous logit space $z\\in\\mathbb{R}^{V}$, where internal perturbations (arising from quantization errors, dropout, or layer norm fluctuations) act additively:\nL576: \nL577:  | $$z_{obs}=z_{true}+\\epsilon,$$  |  | (27)\nL578: where $\\epsilon$ represents stochastic noise in the latent representation. However, the final probability distribution is generated via the Softmax function $p_{i}\\propto e^{z_{i}}$. Consequently, an additive perturbation in the logit space manifests as a multiplicative factor in the probability space:\nL579: \nL580:  | $$p_{obs}\\propto e^{z_{obs}}=e^{z_{true}+\\epsilon}=e^{z_{true}}\\cdot e^{\\epsilon}=p_{true}\\cdot\\xi,$$  |  | (28)\nL581: where $\\xi=e^{\\epsilon}$. This structural property inherent to LLMs dictates that the magnitude of noise scales proportionally with the probability magnitude itself, establishing the multiplicative nature of the uncertainty.\nL582: ##### Scale Constraints of the Probability Simplex.\nL583: \nL584: Assuming an additive noise model (e.g., $p_{obs}=p_{true}+\\delta$, where $\\delta$ is fixed) leads to physical inconsistencies, particularly for the long-tail distribution of tokens. Consider a rare token with a true probability $p_{true}\\approx 10^{-9}$. A standard fixed additive noise (e.g., $\\delta=10^{-4}$) would imply that:\nL585: \nL586:   * •\nL587: \nL588: The observed probability could shift to negative values ($p<0$), violating the axioms of probability.\nL589: \nL590:   * •\nL591: A negligible noise term could arbitrarily increase the probability of a rare token by orders of magnitude (e.g., from $10^{-9}$ to $10^{-4}$), effectively destroying the semantic structure of the language model.\nL592: \nL593: In contrast, a multiplicative noise model ($p_{obs}=p_{true}\\cdot\\xi$) inherently respects the boundary conditions of the probability simplex. It ensures that rare events remain rare under stochastic fluctuation, preserving the order of magnitude for both high-frequency and low-frequency tokens.\nL594: #### A.4.2 Exact Derivation via Group Isomorphism\nL595: \n"
